{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1504685483,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyNTU1MTcwODU=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1796","origin":"github"},"title":"Rethink metadata parsers","message":"As noted in #1630 the metadata parser setup is no longer optimal for what we need to do.\n\nHere are a few thoughts on how it should change:\n\nParsers should have three major modes of operation:\n- report dataset-global metadata (this is what they do now)\n- report metadata for a given list of dataset components (files/directories)\n- dump all relevant metadata in a format that is optimal for query\n   - this mode is needed for aggregation\n   - homogenizing file metadata during aggregation is undesirable, as we would have to \"expand\" any metadata in a per-file format and possibly inflate it dramatically\n   - instead I propose to let each parser chose an optimal dump format that only itself needs to be able to understand. this dump is than put into a metadata object (see #1630) and used to perform file-based queries on aggregated metadata, when needed\n- consequently parser should be able to operate on an existing dataset, or on a set of metadata object files, and be able to perform the same operations","files":null}]}